Format: https://www.debian.org/doc/packaging-manuals/copyright-format/1.0/
Upstream-Name: patrone-notepad
Source: https://github.com/billsecond/patroneos
Comment:
 Notepad links dynamically to GTK 4, GLib, Pango, Cairo, HarfBuzz,
 graphene and the libraries they use, from Ubuntu's own packages
 (libgtk-4-1, libglib2.0-0t64, libpango-1.0-0, libcairo2 and the rest),
 under the LGPL-2.1+ and other free licences. It ships none of them: each
 package's licence and copyright notices are in its own
 /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE/copyright, and its source is in Ubuntu's archive
 (apt-get source PACKAGE).
 .
 It uses PatroneOS's fonts from fonts-patroneos, which it depends on and
 doesn't ship: they're under the SIL Open Font License 1.1
 (/usr/share/doc/fonts-patroneos/OFL.txt).
 .
 Notepad's About window (Settings, About) shows these licences, and the
 full texts in third-party-licenses.txt.gz (below).

Files: *
Copyright: 2026 William Daugherty
License: GPL-3+

Files: vendor/*
Copyright: The authors of each crate
License: MIT or Apache-2.0
Comment:
 The 69 Rust crates in vendor/, which packaging/build.sh adds, are compiled
 into the program, with Rust's standard library. 66 are MIT, or MIT or
 Apache-2.0 at the user's choice; the others are the Unlicense or MIT
 (memchr), Apache-2.0 with the LLVM exception (target-lexicon), and MIT or
 Apache-2.0 with Unicode-3.0 for the Unicode data (unicode-ident). All of
 them allow being part of a GPL-3+ program. The licence texts are below;
 each crate's own licence files, with its copyright notices, and the
 standard library's, are in
 /usr/share/doc/patrone-notepad/third-party-licenses.txt.gz.

